          "content": {
            "size": 4849,
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "[{\"id\":83203,\"id_parent\":15953,\"type_id\":2,\"user_id\":2891,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"step\":1,\"active\":false,\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:02:00.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:02:30.000000Z\",\"score\":0,\"content\":{\"id\":15953,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"title\":\"Information Sources and Critical Appraisal of Clinical Studies\",\"description\":\"\",\"document\":\"2891_22390_1773136950.pdf\",\"document_path\":\"https:\\/\\/api.mooc.oshsu.kg\\/public\\/teacher\\/files\\/lesson\\/documents\\/2891_22390_1773136950.pdf\",\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:02:30.000000Z\"},\"chills\":false,\"count_attempt\":0,\"my_score\":0,\"type\":{\"id\":2,\"title\":\"\\u0414\\u043e\\u043a\\u0443\\u043c\\u0435\\u043d\\u0442\",\"modelName\":\"Document\",\"logo\":\"pi pi-folder\",\"name\":\"document\",\"active\":true,\"created_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:31:58.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:32:01.000000Z\",\"order\":1}},{\"id\":83204,\"id_parent\":38359,\"type_id\":4,\"user_id\":2891,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"step\":2,\"active\":false,\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:02:44.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:04:31.000000Z\",\"score\":1,\"content\":{\"id\":38359,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"content\":\"In a clinical trial investigating a new statin, the results showed a relative risk of myocardial infarction of 0.82 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.68\\u20130.99. Which of the following interpretations is the most accurate?\\n \",\"score\":1,\"image\":null,\"answers\":[{\"id\":145694,\"text\":\"The drug definitely reduces the risk of myocardial infarction by exactly 18%.\"},{\"id\":145695,\"text\":\"The true effect of the drug is most likely somewhere between a 32% reduction and a 1% reduction in risk, and the result is clinically significant.\"},{\"id\":145696,\"text\":\"The probability that the drug reduces the risk is exactly 95%.\"},{\"id\":145697,\"text\":\"The true effect of the drug is most likely somewhere between a 32% reduction and a 1% reduction in risk, and the result is statistically significant.\"}],\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:04:31.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:04:31.000000Z\"},\"chills\":false,\"count_attempt\":0,\"my_score\":0,\"type\":{\"id\":4,\"title\":\"\\u0422\\u0435\\u0441\\u0442\",\"modelName\":\"Test\",\"logo\":\"pi pi-list-check\",\"name\":\"test\",\"active\":true,\"created_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:31:58.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:32:01.000000Z\",\"order\":4}},{\"id\":83209,\"id_parent\":38361,\"type_id\":4,\"user_id\":2891,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"step\":3,\"active\":false,\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:04:39.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:05:59.000000Z\",\"score\":1,\"content\":{\"id\":38361,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"content\":\"In a clinical trial investigating a new antibiotic for pneumonia, complications occurred in 20% of patients receiving standard therapy and in 10% of patients receiving the new drug. Which of the following interpretations is the most accurate?\\n\",\"score\":1,\"image\":null,\"answers\":[{\"id\":145702,\"text\":\"The NNT is 10, meaning ten patients must be treated with the new drug instead of the standard therapy to prevent one additional complication.\"},{\"id\":145703,\"text\":\"The NNT is 10, meaning the drug will prevent complications in 10% of all treated patients.\"},{\"id\":145704,\"text\":\"The NNT is 0.10, indicating the probability that a patient benefits from treatment.\"},{\"id\":145705,\"text\":\"The NNT indicates the relative risk reduction of the treatment.\"}],\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:05:59.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:05:59.000000Z\"},\"chills\":false,\"count_attempt\":0,\"my_score\":0,\"type\":{\"id\":4,\"title\":\"\\u0422\\u0435\\u0441\\u0442\",\"modelName\":\"Test\",\"logo\":\"pi pi-list-check\",\"name\":\"test\",\"active\":true,\"created_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:31:58.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:32:01.000000Z\",\"order\":4}},{\"id\":83214,\"id_parent\":38363,\"type_id\":4,\"user_id\":2891,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"step\":4,\"active\":false,\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:06:05.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:07:21.000000Z\",\"score\":1,\"content\":{\"id\":38363,\"lesson_id\":22390,\"content\":\"A study finds that a new treatment reduces symptom severity with a p-value of 0.04. Which of the following statements correctly describes this p-value?\\n\",\"score\":1,\"image\":null,\"answers\":[{\"id\":145710,\"text\":\"The probability that the null hypothesis is true, given the observed data, is exactly 4%.\"},{\"id\":145711,\"text\":\"If there is truly no difference between treatments, the probability of seeing this result or more extreme is 4%.\"},{\"id\":145712,\"text\":\"The probability that the observed results are due to random sampling error is less than 4%.\"},{\"id\":145713,\"text\":\"There is a 96% chance that repeating the study would yield the same statistically significant result.\"}],\"created_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:07:21.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-03-10T10:07:21.000000Z\"},\"chills\":false,\"count_attempt\":0,\"my_score\":0,\"type\":{\"id\":4,\"title\":\"\\u0422\\u0435\\u0441\\u0442\",\"modelName\":\"Test\",\"logo\":\"pi pi-list-check\",\"name\":\"test\",\"active\":true,\"created_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:31:58.000000Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-09-02T09:32:01.000000Z\",\"order\":4}}]"
          },
